%% CASE Riverside City Marathon and Festival
The city of Riverside is organising a marathon and a two-day festival on a **fixed date, 20 weeks away**. The project uses a predictive plan with a budget at completion of **$1.2 million**.

Status at the end of week 8:

| Measure | Value |
|---|---|
| Planned value (PV) | $480,000 |
| Earned value (EV) | $420,000 |
| Actual cost (AC) | $400,000 |

- The stage and sound contract is **firm fixed price**; catering is **time and materials with a not-to-exceed (NTE) limit** of $90,000.
- The volunteer coordinator has recruited only **300 of the 500 volunteers** needed.
- Several critical-path activities are behind, and the sponsor has said that funds are available if needed to protect the date.
@@ PR6 | Predictive
How is the project performing at week 8?
A) Behind schedule (SPI 0.875) and under budget (CPI 1.05)
B) Ahead of schedule (SPI 1.14) and over budget (CPI 0.95)
C) Behind schedule (SPI 0.875) and over budget (CPI 0.95)
D) On schedule and on budget, since the variances are small
= A
? SPI = EV / PV = 420,000 / 480,000 = 0.875. CPI = EV / AC = 420,000 / 400,000 = 1.05.

@@ PR8 | Predictive
The event date cannot move. Which schedule recovery approach should the project manager consider first?
A) Crash the delayed critical activities by adding resources, using the funds
B) Fast-track activities across the whole project, including those that are not critical
C) Move the event date by two weeks and inform the city council of the change
D) Apply resource smoothing to the critical-path activities within their float
= A
? With a fixed date, critical activities behind schedule, good cost performance and funds available, crashing critical-path work is appropriate. Non-critical activities do not affect the end date, and smoothing does not shorten the critical path.

@@ PR4 | Predictive
How should the project manager address the volunteer shortfall?
A) Launch targeted recruitment, such as university and club partnerships, and track it
B) Reduce the number of volunteer roles at the event so that 300 volunteers are enough on the day
C) Wait until two weeks before the event, when volunteers traditionally register in large numbers
D) Ask the paid staff to cover all of the volunteer roles in addition to their own duties
= A
? Acquiring resources proactively — with new recruitment channels and progress tracking — addresses the gap early. Waiting or overloading staff increases risk on the day.

@@ PR5 | Predictive
The caterer reports that its hours will exceed the $90,000 not-to-exceed limit. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the remaining work and negotiate any increase through a contract change
B) Pay the additional amount, since time and materials contracts reimburse all hours worked
C) Ask the caterer to continue working and send one combined invoice after the event
D) Terminate the catering contract and find a new caterer for the remaining work
= A
? The NTE limit caps the buyer's obligation; exceeding it requires a formal change. The PM reviews what work remains and negotiates through contract change control.
%% END CASE

@@ PR1 | Predictive
An aircraft modification project must be approved by the aviation authority at several points. Where should these approval reviews be planned?
A) In the integrated plan, as milestones with the preparation time and resources
B) Outside the plan, since the authority's reviews are not under the project team's control
C) Only in the risk register, as the risk that the authority will delay its approval
D) In the closing phase, since approvals are only needed before the aircraft returns to service
= A
? Mandatory external reviews are part of the work and must be planned with their preparation, dependencies and lead times.

@@ PR1 | Agile
A bank's marketing team needs to know when a new mobile feature will be available so that it can plan a campaign. How should the product owner align the two plans?
A) Share the release forecast and agree the campaign date based on its confidence
B) Promise a fixed date to marketing and ask the team to meet it whatever happens
C) Ask marketing to wait until the feature is released before planning the campaign
D) Let marketing choose the release date and add it to the sprint backlog
= A
? Release forecasts with confidence levels let marketing plan realistically while the team retains flexibility.

@@ PR1 | Hybrid
A smart-meter project includes new meter hardware, which must be certified, and a mobile app for customers. How should the project manager tailor the approach?
A) Predictive for the certified hardware, adaptive for the app
B) An adaptive approach for the hardware and a predictive approach for the app
C) A predictive approach for both, since the hardware requires certification
D) An adaptive approach for both, since the app requires frequent feedback
= A
? Hybrid tailoring applies predictive planning where requirements are fixed and certification is needed, and adaptive delivery where feedback drives value.

@@ PR1 | Predictive
What is the main purpose of the configuration management plan?
A) To define how product and document versions are controlled and audited
B) To describe how team members will be configured into the organisation's structure
C) To describe how the project's budget will be configured into control accounts
D) To describe how stakeholders will be configured into communication groups
= A
? The configuration management plan defines how configurable items are identified, versioned, controlled and verified.

@@ PR1 | Agile
Before each release, the team and product owner want to confirm that the product can safely go live. What should they define?
A) Release readiness criteria, such as tests passed and support prepared
B) A rule that the product owner decides on the day based on personal judgment
C) A rule that every release goes live automatically at the end of each sprint
D) A rule that the sponsor tests the product personally before every release
= A
? Explicit release criteria, beyond the sprint-level definition of done, make release decisions consistent and safe.

@@ PR1 | Predictive
While integrating the plans, the project manager finds that the procurement plan expects equipment delivery in May, but the schedule needs it in March. What should the project manager do?
A) Resolve the inconsistency so procurement and schedule align
B) Keep both plans, since each was prepared by a different expert
C) Move all activities that need the equipment to May without analysis
D) Ask the supplier to deliver in March without changing the procurement plan
= A
? Integrating plans means resolving inconsistencies between components before baselining.

@@ PR1 | Hybrid
Eight agile teams and two predictive vendor workstreams must coordinate a major platform launch. Which planning practice helps align them?
A) A regular joint planning event to align objectives and dependencies
B) Each team planning independently and sharing its plan by email afterwards
C) A detailed master schedule that lists every story from every team
D) Planning only once at the start of the programme and never revisiting it
= A
? Regular joint planning (similar to PI planning) aligns multiple teams and workstreams on objectives and dependencies.

@@ PR1 | Predictive | multi
Which three baselines make up the performance measurement baseline? (Choose three.)
A) Scope baseline
B) Schedule baseline
C) Cost baseline
D) Risk baseline
E) Quality baseline
= A, B, C
? The performance measurement baseline integrates the scope, schedule and cost baselines and is used for earned value management.

@@ PR2 | Predictive
A bridge project's scope statement does not say who will maintain the bridge after handover. The city assumes the contractor will. What should the project manager have done to prevent this?
A) Stated maintenance explicitly as included or excluded in the scope statement
B) Added maintenance to the risk register as an uncertain future event to monitor
C) Asked the city to assume nothing until the bridge was complete
D) Included the maintenance costs in the management reserve just in case
= A
? Explicit inclusions and exclusions in the scope statement prevent assumptions about what the project delivers.

@@ PR2 | Agile
A story covers the whole loan application process: entering details, uploading documents, credit check and approval. It is too big for one sprint. How should it be split?
A) By workflow step, so each story delivers a usable part
B) By technical layer, such as database, back end and front end
C) By team member, so that each developer gets one part of the story
D) It should not be split; the sprint should be extended to fit it
= A
? Splitting along workflow steps produces smaller stories that each deliver value and can be tested.

@@ PR2 | Hybrid
In a hybrid banking project, some features must meet regulatory requirements and be traceable to them. How should the team ensure this traceability?
A) Keep a traceability matrix linking regulations to backlog items and tests
B) Rely on the product owner's memory of which stories relate to the regulations
C) Document the traceability once, after the final release has been completed
D) Avoid adaptive delivery for any feature linked to a regulation
= A
? A traceability matrix connects requirements to their implementation and verification, which is essential for compliance in any approach.

@@ PR2 | Predictive
An aircraft component must pass a qualification test witnessed by the customer before it is accepted. Which process does this support?
A) Validate Scope
B) Define Scope
C) Plan Scope Management
D) Develop Scope Structure
= A
? Witnessed qualification tests lead to formal acceptance of deliverables, which is the purpose of Validate Scope.

@@ PR2 | Agile
Which factors should the product owner consider when ordering the product backlog?
A) Value, risk, dependencies and learning opportunities
B) Only the size of each item, smallest first
C) Only the order in which stakeholders submitted items
D) Only the developers' technical preferences
= A
? Backlog ordering balances business value with risk reduction, dependencies and what the team needs to learn.

@@ PR2 | Predictive
Several stakeholders have each asked for "minor" additions, which together add significant work. None went through change control. What should the project manager do?
A) Route all of the additions through change control and log them
B) Accept the additions, since each request is small when viewed on its own
C) Reject all of the additions without assessing their value or impact
D) Ask the team to complete the additions in their spare time
= A
? Many small uncontrolled additions are scope creep. All changes should be assessed and decided through change control.

@@ PR2 | Agile
The product must respond within one second for 95% of requests. How should this non-functional requirement be handled in the backlog?
A) As a definition-of-done constraint or acceptance criteria on relevant stories
B) As a separate story to be tested only once, at the end of the release
C) As a note in the product vision statement, without any acceptance criteria
D) As an operational issue to be addressed after the product goes live
= A
? Non-functional requirements apply across stories; they are built into the definition of done or acceptance criteria so they are verified continuously.

@@ PR2 | Hybrid | match
Match each scope artifact with its description.
1) Product backlog
2) Work breakdown structure
3) Sprint backlog
4) Project scope statement
A) An ordered list of all the work that may be needed for the product
B) A hierarchical decomposition of the total scope of work
C) The items selected for a sprint plus the plan to deliver them
D) A description of the scope, deliverables, acceptance criteria and exclusions
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Hybrid projects may use all four artifacts for different components and levels of planning.

@@ PR3 | Agile
A bank's product owner must choose between a feature that customers value highly and one that reduces a major technical risk. How should the backlog be ordered?
A) By weighing value and risk reduction together, possibly tackling the risk early
B) Always by customer value alone, since technical risks are the team's concern
C) Always by risk alone, since risks must be eliminated before any value is delivered
D) By the order in which the items were added to the backlog
= A
? Value-based prioritisation includes risk reduction; tackling major risks early can protect future value.

@@ PR3 | Predictive
The benefits management plan lists target benefits and metrics but does not name anyone responsible for realising them. What is missing?
A) A benefit owner
B) A change control board
C) A risk owner
D) A product owner
= A
? Each benefit needs a benefit owner who is accountable for realising and measuring it, often after the project ends.

@@ PR3 | Hybrid
A new system for 80 bank branches is ready. How can the project start realising benefits as early as possible while managing risk?
A) Roll out to branches in waves, starting with a pilot group
B) Wait until all 80 branches are trained before switching any branch over
C) Switch all 80 branches over on the same weekend
D) Let each branch decide whether to use the system
= A
? Incremental rollout realises benefits sooner and allows lessons from early waves to reduce risk in later ones.

@@ PR3 | Agile
A regulatory deadline means a feature is worthless if delivered after 1 March, while other features lose value gradually. How should this affect prioritisation?
A) It has a high cost of delay, so prioritise it to meet the date
B) All features should be prioritised equally, since every feature has some value
C) The time-critical feature should be dropped, since deadlines create risk
D) The feature should be delivered after 1 March to reduce pressure on the team
= A
? Cost of delay reflects how value changes over time; a hard deadline makes the cost of delay very high.

@@ PR3 | Predictive
A public safety project will not generate revenue. How should its business case express value?
A) Using non-financial benefits, such as fewer accidents, with costs
B) It cannot have a business case, since there is no revenue
C) Using only the project's budget, since value cannot be measured
D) Using the number of deliverables, since benefits are intangible
= A
? Value includes non-financial benefits such as safety and public good; business cases can quantify them with appropriate measures.

@@ PR3 | Agile
Before building a new feature, the product owner wants to make sure its value can be verified. What should be defined first?
A) A hypothesis and the metric that will show success
